WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.984
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4399.

2
00:00:00.984 --> 00:00:01.969
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.969 --> 00:00:10.172
<v DOUGLAS MUNGIN>Alright, today is January 23rd, and I am Douglas Mungin representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

4
00:00:10.172 --> 00:00:16.734
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

5
00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:23.953
And to kick that off we are holding Mossville Oral History Day, here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

6
00:00:23.953 --> 00:00:25.266
I'm here today with . . .

7
00:00:25.266 --> 00:00:25.922
<v JOSEPH PAYNE>Joseph Payne.

8
00:00:25.922 --> 00:00:28.219
<v MUNGIN>Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

9
00:00:28.219 --> 00:00:32.484
This is going to be a short interview of about fifteen minutes long,

10
00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:38.719
and I will likely get in touch with you for a longer interview in the upcoming weeks and months.

11
00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:39.047
Okay.

12
00:00:39.047 --> 00:00:39.375
Alright.

13
00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:42.000
Could you please state your full name?

14
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.239
<v PAYNE>Joseph Eddie Payne.

15
00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:46.130
<v MUNGIN>And when and where were you born?

16
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.848
<v PAYNE>I was born April 6, 1946 in Carencro, Louisiana

17
00:00:49.848 --> 00:00:51.913
<v MUNGIN>And where is Carencro, Louisiana?

18
00:00:51.913 --> 00:00:52.739
Where's that?

19
00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:55.630
<v PAYNE>Right on the outskirts, north of Lafayette.

20
00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:56.043
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

21
00:00:56.043 --> 00:00:58.935
<v PAYNE>Maybe three or four miles from Lafayette.

22
00:00:58.935 --> 00:01:01.413
<v MUNGIN>And were your parents born there?

23
00:01:01.413 --> 00:01:04.717
<v PAYNE>Parents was born there, went to school there.

24
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:15.043
My mother went to school there for sure, and my dad, he didn't get much of an education, but he grew up in Carencro area.

25
00:01:15.043 --> 00:01:20.000
<v MUNGIN>And your grandparents came there or they were also born there?

26
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.030
<v PAYNE>Grandparents was in Lafayette, Louisiana.

27
00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:30.303
He was . . . my grandfather was a railroad man back in that day.

28
00:01:30.303 --> 00:01:36.364
My grandfather . . . My grandmother she was a regular blind lady.

29
00:01:36.364 --> 00:01:42.424
I grew up with my grandmother, and she was blind.

30
00:01:42.424 --> 00:01:48.485
And she always needed somebody to be there with her.

31
00:01:48.485 --> 00:01:57.576
And we grew up with her and learned to cook with her and enjoyed Carencro.

32
00:01:57.576 --> 00:02:09.697
And I was a little kid, got ran over, followed my brother, and had both . . . Well, my leg was broke.

33
00:02:09.697 --> 00:02:12.121
<v MUNGIN>How old were you?

34
00:02:12.121 --> 00:02:13.939
<v PAYNE>Five years old.

35
00:02:13.939 --> 00:02:17.576
<v MUNGIN>What did you get hit by?

36
00:02:17.576 --> 00:02:18.788
<v PAYNE>A car.

37
00:02:18.788 --> 00:02:20.000
<v MUNGIN>Where?

38
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.174
<v PAYNE>On a gravel road in Carencro, crossing the road, following my older brother.

39
00:02:26.174 --> 00:02:27.599
<v MUNGIN>Following to what?

40
00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:33.773
<v PAYNE>They was leaving the house and I wanted to go with them [laughs].

41
00:02:33.773 --> 00:02:40.896
And I got hit by a car, and I was . . . I got my leg broke,

42
00:02:40.896 --> 00:02:50.870
and they had to re-break it again after they found out . . . They had it in a cast, and they x-rayed it.

43
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:56.094
And then they took the cast off, and it was crooked.

44
00:02:56.094 --> 00:03:04.642
Doc said they had to break it again and reset it and said, possibly, I'd never walk again.

45
00:03:04.642 --> 00:03:16.040
And I ended up playing football, going to the military on that broke leg and seeing like today it's giving me the trouble now.

46
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.739
<v MUNGIN>[laughs] It took all that time to finally give you some trouble.

47
00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:22.214
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

48
00:03:22.214 --> 00:03:25.064
<v MUNGIN>So what were your parents' names?

49
00:03:25.064 --> 00:03:28.388
<v PAYNE>My daddy was Wilson Payne, a bricklayer.

50
00:03:28.388 --> 00:03:35.512
He had no education but [was] the best bricklayer in this part of the country.

51
00:03:35.512 --> 00:03:43.110
My mother, she worked odd jobs at the restaurants, since we was here in Lake Charles.

52
00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.435
We moved from Lafayette to Lake Charles.

53
00:03:46.435 --> 00:03:52.134
We was, well my parents. . . He was a fine bricklayer, good bricklayer.

54
00:03:52.134 --> 00:03:56.883
<v MUNGIN>Did he work for a company or was he just . . .

55
00:03:56.883 --> 00:03:58.783
<v PAYNE>He worked for himself.

56
00:03:58.783 --> 00:04:04.007
He was a helper with another bricklayer, that's how he learned.

57
00:04:04.007 --> 00:04:12.555
He was a helper for a bricklayer, and the bricklayer said, "Man, you always on my scaffold, man.

58
00:04:12.555 --> 00:04:20.154
Won't you go get you a house and build your own . . . work on your own house?"

59
00:04:20.154 --> 00:04:23.003
And that's what my daddy did.

60
00:04:23.003 --> 00:04:30.602
He started working for himself, and he ended up being the best bricklayer in this area.

61
00:04:30.602 --> 00:04:32.977
<v MUNGIN>What were your grandparents' names?

62
00:04:32.977 --> 00:04:36.776
<v PAYNE>George Bernard and my grandmother was Josephine Bernard.

63
00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:42.000
They also lived . . . that’s where they come up in Carencro.

64
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.088
<v MUNGIN>So when did you move to Mossville?

65
00:04:45.088 --> 00:04:49.059
<v PAYNE>We came to Lake Charles first and stayed there.

66
00:04:49.059 --> 00:04:53.912
<v MUNGIN>About how old were you when you came to Lake Charles?

67
00:04:53.912 --> 00:04:55.676
<v PAYNE>About six years old.

68
00:04:55.676 --> 00:04:58.324
<v MUNGIN>How many siblings did you have?

69
00:04:58.324 --> 00:05:00.971
<v PAYNE>Ended up being thirteen of us.

70
00:05:00.971 --> 00:05:01.412
<v MUNGIN>Thirteen?

71
00:05:01.412 --> 00:05:02.294
<v PAYNE>[laughs] Yeah.

72
00:05:02.294 --> 00:05:07.147
But at the time, I was about the third, fourth oldest.

73
00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:09.353
<v MUNGIN>How many boys and girls?

74
00:05:09.353 --> 00:05:12.000
<v PAYNE>Nine boys and four girls.

75
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.640
<v MUNGIN>That's a lot of boys. [laughs]

76
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:15.080
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

77
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:20.800
And we made a name for ourselves, too. [laughs] We were football players.

78
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.320
Enjoyed it and . . . good members of the community.

79
00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.720
<v MUNGIN>So you were six when you moved to Lake Charles.

80
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.000
What age, how old were you when you moved to Mossville?

81
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.000
<v PAYNE>Possibly seven.

82
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.000
About seven years old.

83
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:42.000
<v MUNGIN>Why did your family move from Lake Charles to Mossville?

84
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:50.500
<v PAYNE>I had an uncle live here, and he had, he knew of property right behind his house.

85
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.500
We moved from . . . well we bought property over in Mossville.

86
00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:07.500
I think that was some of the money that, from the [law]suit they had when I was a kid, when I got run over.

87
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:13.000
<v MUNGIN>So you, your family sued the people than ran you over?

88
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:13.500
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

89
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.500
<v MUNGIN>How much was the suit for?

90
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.500
<v PAYNE>It wasn't a lot of money.

91
00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.500
Couple of thousands, maybe.

92
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.000
<v MUNGIN>And was property pretty cheap back then in Mossville?

93
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:26.500
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

94
00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:30.000
<v MUNGIN>What's your earliest memory of Mossville?

95
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.022
<v PAYNE>First moving to Mossville, that's my first memory of it. [laughs]

96
00:06:35.022 --> 00:06:37.761
<v MUNGIN>So what did it look like?

97
00:06:37.761 --> 00:06:40.957
<v PAYNE>It was wood, all over the place.

98
00:06:40.957 --> 00:06:51.000
It was the main drag of Prater Road, right across the track, was a little neighborhood with about two or three houses.

99
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.196
Behind there, that's where we moved to.

100
00:06:54.196 --> 00:07:05.152
Queensboro, another subdivision across the street from, as you cross the railroad track on Prater, they hadn't even sold a lot over there yet.

101
00:07:05.152 --> 00:07:10.630
I remember the time when they was having the sale over there.

102
00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:20.217
We was on this side of the track, and the sale was going on on the other side of the road.

103
00:07:20.217 --> 00:07:22.957
It was nothing but woods there.

104
00:07:22.957 --> 00:07:28.435
<v MUNGIN>So it was a pretty small community when you first moved here.

105
00:07:28.435 --> 00:07:28.891
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

106
00:07:28.891 --> 00:07:33.000
<v MUNGIN>And so, when did people start moving in?

107
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:33.645
<v PAYNE>Oh.

108
00:07:33.645 --> 00:07:38.161
People always . . . it was always moving in.

109
00:07:38.161 --> 00:07:43.968
Especially when that little neighborhood opened up and then . . .

110
00:07:43.968 --> 00:07:45.903
<v MUNGIN>What little neighborhood?

111
00:07:45.903 --> 00:07:46.548
<v PAYNE>Queensboro.

112
00:07:46.548 --> 00:07:47.194
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

113
00:07:47.194 --> 00:07:49.774
<v BILL SHEARMAN>That was the subdivision?

114
00:07:49.774 --> 00:07:53.000
<v PAYNE>Yes sir, the subdivision.

115
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:57.308
<v MUNGIN>And then that's when the community started to get bigger and bigger.

116
00:07:57.308 --> 00:07:59.821
<v PAYNE>Yeah, that's what it seemed to me.

117
00:07:59.821 --> 00:08:02.692
And they had the night club over there.

118
00:08:02.692 --> 00:08:05.564
<v MUNGIN>What was the name of the night club?

119
00:08:05.564 --> 00:08:06.282
<v PAYNE>The Paradise.

120
00:08:06.282 --> 00:08:09.154
<v MUNGIN>[laughs] Was it a pretty happening night club?

121
00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:09.872
<v PAYNE>Oh, yeah.

122
00:08:09.872 --> 00:08:16.333
It was the biggest and the nicest night club in this area, even in the Lake Charles area.

123
00:08:16.333 --> 00:08:25.308
It was only maybe one or two big night clubs over there, but we had a pretty big night club over here in Mossville also.

124
00:08:25.308 --> 00:08:27.103
<v MUNGIN>When did it close down?

125
00:08:27.103 --> 00:08:30.333
<v PAYNE>[pauses] I would say about twenty years ago now.

126
00:08:30.333 --> 00:08:32.846
It's been a good while ago now.

127
00:08:32.846 --> 00:08:35.000
<v MUNGIN>Why did it close down?

128
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.216
<v PAYNE>Oh. [pauses] I don't know.

129
00:08:37.216 --> 00:08:40.763
If the guy, if Mr. Valery Montgomery was . . .

130
00:08:40.763 --> 00:08:42.979
<v MUNGIN>Was that the owner's name?

131
00:08:42.979 --> 00:08:43.423
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

132
00:08:43.423 --> 00:08:43.866
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

133
00:08:43.866 --> 00:08:51.402
<v PAYNE>I don't know if he was getting elderly or what, but he did shut the place down.

134
00:08:51.402 --> 00:08:54.505
<v SHEARMAN>Did you say Val Montgomery, Mr. Payne?

135
00:08:54.505 --> 00:08:55.392
<v PAYNE>Valery Montgomery.

136
00:08:55.392 --> 00:09:01.155
<v MUNGIN>So when you were growing up, what . . . where did you guys move to?

137
00:09:01.155 --> 00:09:03.371
What was the street, again?

138
00:09:03.371 --> 00:09:04.258
I'm sorry.

139
00:09:04.258 --> 00:09:08.247
<v PAYNE>Yeah, we moved right across the street from here.

140
00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:10.464
<v MUNGIN>Okay, what street is that?

141
00:09:10.464 --> 00:09:11.794
<v PAYNE>Old Spanish Trail.

142
00:09:11.794 --> 00:09:12.680
<v MUNGIN>Oh, okay.

143
00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.897
Could you describe the house?

144
00:09:14.897 --> 00:09:18.000
How did it look back then?

145
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:24.476
<v PAYNE>It was like . . . It wasn't a sharpshooter, it was much wider than a sharpshooter.

146
00:09:24.476 --> 00:09:28.176
But it was a long, straight house and . . .

147
00:09:28.176 --> 00:09:31.414
<v MUNGIN>So was it like a shotgun house?

148
00:09:31.414 --> 00:09:31.877
Or . . .?

149
00:09:31.877 --> 00:09:42.053
<v PAYNE>It was bedrooms on one side, and the living room . . . dining room and kitchen on one side, and bedrooms on one side.

150
00:09:42.053 --> 00:09:45.291
It was about three or four bedrooms.

151
00:09:45.291 --> 00:09:48.529
And I always stayed with my grandmother.

152
00:09:48.529 --> 00:09:56.392
My grandmother lived right behind the family house, and that's the family house right across the street.

153
00:09:56.392 --> 00:10:02.868
I remember when they first built this swimming pool across the street over here.

154
00:10:02.868 --> 00:10:11.194
Man, we was swimming out there, man, they had brought the bulldozers over there and they dragged line,

155
00:10:11.194 --> 00:10:15.819
and they started digging, and there come a big rain.

156
00:10:15.819 --> 00:10:25.533
And . . . we was swimming out there in the little mud hole they had dug. [laugh] Before the swimming pool was built.

157
00:10:25.533 --> 00:10:27.846
<v SHEARMAN>That was fifty-five, Mr. Payne?

158
00:10:27.846 --> 00:10:28.308
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

159
00:10:28.308 --> 00:10:30.159
A long time ago.

160
00:10:30.159 --> 00:10:36.172
<v MUNGIN>So . . . Did your . . . did the property have a lot of land around it,

161
00:10:36.172 --> 00:10:40.335
and what did you guys do with that land?

162
00:10:40.335 --> 00:10:45.885
<v PAYNE>It was constantly being developed for more houses and more houses and . . .

163
00:10:45.885 --> 00:10:47.736
<v MUNGIN>Your piece of land?

164
00:10:47.736 --> 00:10:48.198
Or . . .

165
00:10:48.198 --> 00:10:50.974
<v PAYNE>Oh, what, the family house land?

166
00:10:50.974 --> 00:10:53.286
<v MUNGIN>Yeah the family land, yeah.

167
00:10:53.286 --> 00:10:57.912
<v PAYNE>Oh, no. It wasn't that big of a land. [laughs]

168
00:10:57.912 --> 00:11:03.000
<v MUNGIN>Did y'all have like a garden or anything like that?

169
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.308
<v PAYNE>Oh yeah, we had gardens.

170
00:11:05.308 --> 00:11:06.231
Oh man.

171
00:11:06.231 --> 00:11:10.385
Big berry trees, fig trees, all of this, man.

172
00:11:10.385 --> 00:11:12.231
All in the yard.

173
00:11:12.231 --> 00:11:15.462
<v MUNGIN>So your father was like a bricklayer.

174
00:11:15.462 --> 00:11:21.923
Did your family have like have any hobbies or anything that you guys did?

175
00:11:21.923 --> 00:11:27.000
Like with your father, or your mom, or your grandma?

176
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.148
<v PAYNE>We . . . We always worked with my dad.

177
00:11:31.148 --> 00:11:34.111
Enjoyed learning to lay bricks.

178
00:11:34.111 --> 00:11:43.000
I would have probably been his best bricklayer next to my oldest brother here for,

179
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:56.630
if I would have stayed in this area, but I graduated and went to the military, and I was in the air force.

180
00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:00.778
<v MUNGIN>So what are your siblings' names again?

181
00:12:00.778 --> 00:12:02.556
<v PAYNE>From start to . . .

182
00:12:02.556 --> 00:12:03.148
<v MUNGIN>[laughs]

183
00:12:03.148 --> 00:12:05.519
<v PAYNE>From top to bottom?

184
00:12:05.519 --> 00:12:10.259
<v MUNGIN>[laughing] I know that's a lot of them

185
00:12:10.259 --> 00:12:13.222
<v PAYNE>Alvin, Alvin was the oldest.

186
00:12:13.222 --> 00:12:16.778
Alvin, Ella, Wilford, Joseph, that's me.

187
00:12:16.778 --> 00:12:21.519
Leo, Dorothy, Helen, Larry . . . Did I name Sunny?

188
00:12:21.519 --> 00:12:26.259
<v MUNGIN>One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight.

189
00:12:26.259 --> 00:12:29.815
<v PAYNE>I got Sunny down on there?

190
00:12:29.815 --> 00:12:31.000
<v MUNGIN>No.

191
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.692
<v PAYNE>Well, Sunny.

192
00:12:32.692 --> 00:12:35.231
I got Dorothy?

193
00:12:35.231 --> 00:12:36.077
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

194
00:12:36.077 --> 00:12:37.769
<v PAYNE>Dorothy, Helen.

195
00:12:37.769 --> 00:12:38.615
<v MUNGIN>Yes.

196
00:12:38.615 --> 00:12:44.538
Someone's going to be upset with you.

197
00:12:44.538 --> 00:12:47.923
<v PAYNE>Calvin, David, and Barbara.

198
00:12:47.923 --> 00:12:53.000
<v MUNGIN>Alright, you knocked it out.

199
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.957
<v PAYNE>Okay, that's all of it?

200
00:12:54.957 --> 00:12:55.739
<v MUNGIN>Yeah [laughs].

201
00:12:55.739 --> 00:12:57.304
<v PAYNE>Keeping up with them.

202
00:12:57.304 --> 00:13:00.043
<v MUNGIN>So where did you go to school?

203
00:13:00.043 --> 00:13:00.826
<v PAYNE>Oh, Mossville.

204
00:13:00.826 --> 00:13:01.217
<v MUNGIN>Mossville?

205
00:13:01.217 --> 00:13:02.391
Where was that?

206
00:13:02.391 --> 00:13:03.174
Right here?

207
00:13:03.174 --> 00:13:04.739
<v PAYNE>Mossville down the street.

208
00:13:04.739 --> 00:13:07.870
About a mile, half a mile from here.

209
00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:14.522
Just where the Mossville, the school . . . was first an elementary and before they built the high school.

210
00:13:14.522 --> 00:13:23.522
It was right across the street from my house and I was late for school most of time, living right across the street.

211
00:13:23.522 --> 00:13:25.870
<v MUNGIN>So the elementary school was here?

212
00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:26.261
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

213
00:13:26.261 --> 00:13:29.000
<v MUNGIN>So where was the high school?

214
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:36.950
<v PAYNE>They didn't have a high school right at the time when the school first opened, the elementary.

215
00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:40.223
And what was that, in fifty five?

216
00:13:40.223 --> 00:13:43.029
I think it was, with Mossville.

217
00:13:43.029 --> 00:13:45.367
They got the high school.

218
00:13:45.367 --> 00:13:54.719
And . . . That's when they had the football team, a good football team, out of all the schools in the area.

219
00:13:54.719 --> 00:14:02.669
It was Vinton kids came to high school in Mossville, Sulphur kids went to school at Mossville.

220
00:14:02.669 --> 00:14:04.072
Westlake and Mossville.

221
00:14:04.072 --> 00:14:12.489
We had a heck of a team out of all of those surrounding areas. [Payne and Mungin laugh]

222
00:14:12.489 --> 00:14:15.295
<v MUNGIN>So what year did you graduate?

223
00:14:15.295 --> 00:14:15.763
<v PAYNE>Sixty-seven.

224
00:14:15.763 --> 00:14:16.230
<v MUNGIN>Sixty-seven?

225
00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:20.439
So you decided to go into the air force?

226
00:14:20.439 --> 00:14:20.906
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

227
00:14:20.906 --> 00:14:25.583
<v MUNGIN>Okay, how long were you in the air force for?

228
00:14:25.583 --> 00:14:26.518
<v PAYNE>Four years.

229
00:14:26.518 --> 00:14:26.986
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

230
00:14:26.986 --> 00:14:30.727
What was your job in the air force?

231
00:14:30.727 --> 00:14:33.065
<v PAYNE>I was a civil engineer.

232
00:14:33.065 --> 00:14:34.000
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

233
00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:40.212
<v PAYNE>In the air force, I think, it was almost like next to going to college, man.

234
00:14:40.212 --> 00:14:42.153
Going into the air force.

235
00:14:42.153 --> 00:14:44.094
<v MUNGIN>Why do you say that?

236
00:14:44.094 --> 00:14:53.024
<v PAYNE>Because I learned a craft in there and just about any job you have in the air force, you will learn a craft.

237
00:14:53.024 --> 00:14:56.129
Something you can use when you get out.

238
00:14:56.129 --> 00:15:03.894
And I seen a lot of action, a lot of my friends come from Vietnam and that was the time.

239
00:15:03.894 --> 00:15:07.000
<v MUNGIN>Did you have to go to Vietnam?

240
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:07.747
<v PAYNE>I went.

241
00:15:07.747 --> 00:15:08.121
Yeah.

242
00:15:08.121 --> 00:15:16.717
I saw how all the fellows was coming back, man, and I say, "Man, I don't even want to go to the army.

243
00:15:16.717 --> 00:15:22.323
I know they going to draft me, and I want to make my own decision."

244
00:15:22.323 --> 00:15:29.424
And the first, when I was eighteen, you had to report for the military, and I hadn't graduated yet,

245
00:15:29.424 --> 00:15:35.778
but they told me, they say, "As soon as you graduate from school, we going to be

246
00:15:35.778 --> 00:15:36.899
<c.p>calling you again."

247
00:15:36.899 --> 00:15:44.000
They called me again as soon as I graduated, and I went to take my physical in Houston.

248
00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.311
As soon as I get back,

249
00:15:46.311 --> 00:15:56.709
I didn't even go home and talk to my parents or anybody about me joining the military because I knew that was something I had to do.

250
00:15:56.709 --> 00:16:03.642
I knew they was fixing to get me, so I said, "I'm going to make my own decision."

251
00:16:03.642 --> 00:16:11.730
I talked to all the recruiters in there, and I said, "I'd like to take the test for the air force."

252
00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:19.047
I took the test for the air force, and the guy told me, he said, "You passed the test."

253
00:16:19.047 --> 00:16:20.203
I said, "Wow."

254
00:16:20.203 --> 00:16:24.054
And I said, "I didn't think I'd pass no test."

255
00:16:24.054 --> 00:16:27.520
Because I wasn't a student of testing at all.

256
00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:35.223
And I passed the first important test of my life. [laughs] And . . . I came home and I told my parents,

257
00:16:35.223 --> 00:16:39.074
I said, "I just signed up for the military, Mom."

258
00:16:39.074 --> 00:16:41.770
"Boy, you don't know what you doing!

259
00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:49.858
You see your brother in the Army, and he's . . . he's just a cursing dude, boy, he caught up in the military."

260
00:16:49.858 --> 00:16:53.324
I said, "Well I'm going in the air force."

261
00:16:53.324 --> 00:16:57.561
She said, well she was a little bit relieved with that.

262
00:16:57.561 --> 00:17:02.182
And, in the recruiter actually said, "When do you want to go?"

263
00:17:02.182 --> 00:17:05.264
He said, "Want to go in three months?"

264
00:17:05.264 --> 00:17:11.041
I said, "No, I feel like the military going to get me sooner than that."

265
00:17:11.041 --> 00:17:16.818
He said, "I got one going in a week, you could go in a week."

266
00:17:16.818 --> 00:17:19.128
I said, "Yeah, I do that."

267
00:17:19.128 --> 00:17:22.595
I said, "I'd like to go in a week."

268
00:17:22.595 --> 00:17:31.453
Went home and threw me a party. [laughs] I was ready to go man, and I went into the air force, enjoyed myself.

269
00:17:31.453 --> 00:17:32.993
Enjoyed my four years.

270
00:17:32.993 --> 00:17:36.459
I wish I would've stayed in longer now, but . . .

271
00:17:36.459 --> 00:17:36.845
<v MUNGIN>Why?

272
00:17:36.845 --> 00:17:37.230
<v PAYNE>Huh?

273
00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:38.000
<v MUNGIN>Why?

274
00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:47.283
<v PAYNE>Oh, I could have retired and retired at twenty years. [laughs] But I got out and I was in a good field.

275
00:17:47.283 --> 00:17:49.392
I was in civil engineering.

276
00:17:49.392 --> 00:17:56.565
I was, I was anxious to go back to the Vietnam area to work as a civilian.

277
00:17:56.565 --> 00:18:07.536
Well, they had a lot of, a lot of jobs in Saudi Arabia and all over elsewhere, that's where I said I wanted to go to.

278
00:18:07.536 --> 00:18:08.380
Tax-free money.

279
00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:12.177
<v MUNGIN>Is that what you did once you got out?

280
00:18:12.177 --> 00:18:12.599
<v PAYNE>What?

281
00:18:12.599 --> 00:18:16.397
<v MUNGIN>Is that what you did once you got out?

282
00:18:16.397 --> 00:18:21.460
<v PAYNE>I got out and I went to a heavy equipment school again.

283
00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:26.945
I went to a heavy equipment school when I got out the military.

284
00:18:26.945 --> 00:18:32.008
I took it while I was in California, my last duty station.

285
00:18:32.008 --> 00:18:37.494
And they was passing pamphlets out, and they had a heavy equipment school.

286
00:18:37.494 --> 00:18:39.181
It was in Miami.

287
00:18:39.181 --> 00:18:44.245
Did not know I was going to be going back to Florida.

288
00:18:44.245 --> 00:18:51.418
I took the test and passed everything, and I had to do my residential training over there.

289
00:18:51.418 --> 00:18:56.903
My wife also, I met my wife while I was in the military.

290
00:18:56.903 --> 00:19:00.700
<v MUNGIN>And what was your, what was your wife's name?

291
00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:01.122
<v PAYNE>Vivian.

292
00:19:01.122 --> 00:19:05.342
And I call her my souvenir from the military. [laughs]

293
00:19:05.342 --> 00:19:07.873
<v MUNGIN>And you met her in Miami?

294
00:19:07.873 --> 00:19:08.295
<v PAYNE>Pensacola.

295
00:19:08.295 --> 00:19:08.717
<v MUNGIN>Pensacola?

296
00:19:08.717 --> 00:19:09.139
<v PAYNE>Yes.

297
00:19:09.139 --> 00:19:13.781
I enjoy Pensacola, that's my next best place to being home . . .

298
00:19:13.781 --> 00:19:18.000
<v MUNGIN>So your family's been here since the 1950s, right?

299
00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:18.509
<v PAYNE>Yes.

300
00:19:18.509 --> 00:19:23.091
<v MUNGIN>So how much has this area changed since then?

301
00:19:23.091 --> 00:19:27.164
<v PAYNE>It changed a lot, it changed a lot.

302
00:19:27.164 --> 00:19:35.309
Lot of houses that came into this area before all the refineries taking over right now,

303
00:19:35.309 --> 00:19:46.000
and I know it's time for improvements and time for changes, but we going to miss the old neighborhood. [laughs]

304
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:55.516
<v MUNGIN>So there was a period in time where the population in Mossville, like, started to really spike up.

305
00:19:55.516 --> 00:19:59.217
Like in the 70s and the 80s.

306
00:19:59.217 --> 00:20:00.274
<v PAYNE>Oh yeah.

307
00:20:00.274 --> 00:20:08.204
<v MUNGIN>So why did that population, why did the population start to grow around that time?

308
00:20:08.204 --> 00:20:18.777
<v PAYNE>I think it was just all the family members that was born here, they got married and had kids here,

309
00:20:18.777 --> 00:20:26.178
and wanting to stay in this area, and built it up more and more.

310
00:20:26.178 --> 00:20:31.465
<v MUNGIN>So who else has been some of the community leaders?

311
00:20:31.465 --> 00:20:36.223
Like during that time that you’ve been in Mossville.

312
00:20:36.223 --> 00:20:46.796
<v PAYNE>[pauses] The Rigmaidens, they was a big . . . They was the mayor, had a little mayor of Mossville at one time.

313
00:20:46.796 --> 00:20:48.382
<v MUNGIN>For how long?

314
00:20:48.382 --> 00:20:49.439
<v PAYNE>[pauses] Ten.

315
00:20:49.439 --> 00:20:51.554
Quite a few years.

316
00:20:51.554 --> 00:20:52.611
<v MUNGIN>Oh, okay.

317
00:20:52.611 --> 00:20:55.783
<v PAYNE>They had a little mayor here.

318
00:20:55.783 --> 00:21:05.828
And the when the old, the elderly died, they just branched off to just about nobody being the mayor.

319
00:21:05.828 --> 00:21:09.000
<v SHEARMAN>Was he elected, Mr. Payne?

320
00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:18.986
<v PAYNE>[sighs] I don't know if he was, it was big politic-like that . . . election in this area or what.

321
00:21:18.986 --> 00:21:25.089
But he was considered, he was considered the mayor, Mr. Rigmaiden.

322
00:21:25.089 --> 00:21:25.644
<v SHEARMAN>Josh?

323
00:21:25.644 --> 00:21:26.753
<v PAYNE>Josh Rigmaiden.

324
00:21:26.753 --> 00:21:27.308
Yes.

325
00:21:27.308 --> 00:21:37.849
<v MUNGIN>And what were some of the important places in Mossville, like during that time, like from then until now?

326
00:21:37.849 --> 00:21:48.945
<v PAYNE>[pauses] It's really, it really went down after the elderly people that had it, they had a few stores here.

327
00:21:48.945 --> 00:21:56.712
Ira Garrett and the Braxtons, they had a, they left and moved to California.

328
00:21:56.712 --> 00:22:00.596
They had a good little business down.

329
00:22:00.596 --> 00:22:05.034
They had a store, like a grocery store.

330
00:22:05.034 --> 00:22:08.363
They had a little furniture store.

331
00:22:08.363 --> 00:22:13.356
They had a quite a few rent houses, too.

332
00:22:13.356 --> 00:22:24.452
And a few stores further down this way, down west of Old Spanish Trail here, they had stores over there.

333
00:22:24.452 --> 00:22:30.000
After the elderly died off, the stores went, too.

334
00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:30.533
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

335
00:22:30.533 --> 00:22:34.800
I've got about three more questions for you.

336
00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:38.533
So, what does home mean to you?

337
00:22:38.533 --> 00:22:42.800
What does the term home mean to you?

338
00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:43.333
<v PAYNE>Home.

339
00:22:43.333 --> 00:22:50.800
It's a place that I like to always be able to come back to.

340
00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:59.867
But this going to be one time I never be able to come back to this home.

341
00:22:59.867 --> 00:23:09.467
Not to say it's no type of home, it's going to be refineries all over this place shortly.

342
00:23:09.467 --> 00:23:20.133
I'm kind of looking forward to coming back and even passing through the refineries and still see some familiar places.

343
00:23:20.133 --> 00:23:23.333
<v MUNGIN>What does community mean to you?

344
00:23:23.333 --> 00:23:24.400
<v PAYNE>A community.

345
00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:30.800
That's a place of . . . Where people can visit each other and . . . Because,

346
00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:37.200
I live in a community now where I hardly know my neighbors.

347
00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:42.000
And this neighborhood here, I knew everybody in town.

348
00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.133
Everybody in every house.

349
00:23:44.133 --> 00:23:46.800
Both side of the road.

350
00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:59.067
Right there where I live today, I been living there forty-something years and I don't know my neighbor three houses down from me.

351
00:23:59.067 --> 00:24:07.067
I seen them all my life but, and I know their names but, a visit.

352
00:24:07.067 --> 00:24:10.267
No visitor or none of that.

353
00:24:10.267 --> 00:24:22.000
<v MUNGIN>Why is . . . Why have you been unable to get that kind of get that connection with your neighborhood that you have here?

354
00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.667
<v PAYNE>What, that I left here?

355
00:24:24.667 --> 00:24:30.533
<v MUNGIN>Yeah, like you have your own house in your own neighborhood.

356
00:24:30.533 --> 00:24:38.000
Why haven't you felt like that community there, but you feel it here?

357
00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.089
<v PAYNE>I have no idea.

358
00:24:40.089 --> 00:24:47.924
I don't know if it's the . . . I'm a friendly guy. [laugh] And I don't mind,

359
00:24:47.924 --> 00:24:56.280
I love my neighbors, but I just, we just, they not a talking people or something.

360
00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:59.936
I don't know what's the deal but-

361
00:24:59.936 --> 00:25:03.070
<v MUNGIN>You're all talking people here? [laughs]

362
00:25:03.070 --> 00:25:06.726
<v PAYNE>Yeah, always been a talking person here.

363
00:25:06.726 --> 00:25:10.382
<v SHEARMAN>When did you leave Mossville, Mr. Payne?

364
00:25:10.382 --> 00:25:13.516
<v PAYNE>When I got out the military.

365
00:25:13.516 --> 00:25:19.783
I got out in ‘72, and I stayed here about a year.

366
00:25:19.783 --> 00:25:28.140
And I moved to Lake Charles and I been there ever since, in the same spot.

367
00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:30.752
I never been a moving-around-guy.

368
00:25:30.752 --> 00:25:38.064
Me and the wife, we moved one time since, in our whole entire marriage.

369
00:25:38.064 --> 00:25:38.586
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

370
00:25:38.586 --> 00:25:44.331
<v PAYNE>That right back there in Lake Charles, in the same spot.

371
00:25:44.331 --> 00:25:46.943
<v MUNGIN>One last question for you.

372
00:25:46.943 --> 00:26:00.000
What's the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville that isn't in the history books or folks don't really know about?

373
00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:16.877
<v PAYNE>I would like everybody to know that this has been the best neighborhood and all my friends I had then, they are still my friends today.

374
00:26:16.877 --> 00:26:27.263
I feel like this will, this always be home to me, even though the refineries here,

375
00:26:27.263 --> 00:26:35.702
this the best neighborhood that I could . . . I ever been a part of.

376
00:26:35.702 --> 00:26:46.737
<v MUNGIN>Alright, our time is almost up but is there anything you want to add in the closing?

377
00:26:46.737 --> 00:27:02.965
<v PAYNE>[pauses] Not. . . [pauses] I'm not much of a big talker. [laughs] But I just want everybody to know that this has been a great place,

378
00:27:02.965 --> 00:27:08.807
and I'm willing to let it go to progress.

379
00:27:08.807 --> 00:27:14.000
I know it's in good hands. [laughs]

380
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:21.351
<v MUNGIN>Alright, so that concludes our interview for today but I definitely would love to schedule another interview with you in the next coming weeks,

381
00:27:21.351 --> 00:27:23.495
or in like the next couple months.

382
00:27:23.495 --> 00:27:26.252
Because I definitely want to talk about your cooking.

383
00:27:26.252 --> 00:27:27.784
We haven’t even discussed that.

384
00:27:27.784 --> 00:27:28.703
<v PAYNE>Oh yeah. [laughs]

385
00:27:28.703 --> 00:27:29.009
<v MUNGIN>[laughs]

386
00:27:29.009 --> 00:27:31.153
<v PAYNE>Oh yeah, that's one of my hobbies.

387
00:27:31.153 --> 00:27:34.216
<v MUNGIN>Yeah, we're going to have to talk about that. [laughs]

388
00:27:34.216 --> 00:27:34.829
<v PAYNE>Oh yeah.

389
00:27:34.829 --> 00:27:36.973
Can I bring some of my . . . Well,

390
00:27:36.973 --> 00:27:42.180
I don't know if I can take pictures, because I would have to bring so much stuff.

391
00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:44.018
<v MUNGIN>Well, we'll come to your house.

392
00:27:44.018 --> 00:27:46.775
We can come to your house to interview you.

393
00:27:46.775 --> 00:27:47.081
<v PAYNE>Okay.

394
00:27:47.081 --> 00:27:47.387
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

395
00:27:47.387 --> 00:27:48.000
Cool.

396
00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:48.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4399.

397
00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:48.200
End Session I.]
